MiniMax-H3 video engine (h3): Splash's copy

Imported from h3 commit 5b55ab7 (branch main, ABI v2: h3_prep, h3_denoise,
h3_decode, h3_decode_audio, h3_preview, h3_write_mp4), once, without its
history:

  h3 runtime/{engine,media,model,ops}/   -> runtime/video/h3/{engine,media,model,ops}/
  h3 include/h3/h3.h (C ABI)             -> runtime/video/h3/h3.h
  h3 runtime/metal/abi/                  -> runtime/metal/abi/video/
  h3 runtime/metal/kernels/GROUP/        -> runtime/metal/kernels/video/GROUP/
  h3 tools/convert_*.py, h3/{__init__,common,config,safetensors}.py
                                         -> install/video/

Since then the copy has two parts.

As h3 has them: the kernels, their host/shader ABI headers and the weight
converters, with include paths rewritten for their places ("metal/abi/" ->
"metal/abi/video/", "metal/kernels/common/" -> "metal/kernels/video/common/").
Their arithmetic is h3's: the golden clip of make test-video-real is byte for
byte h3's.

Splash's own: the host code in runtime/video/h3 is the engine's (the C ABI,
request preparation, the DiT scheduler and its memory plan, weight stream and
key-range table, the operators, the decoders, the mp4 writer), but it runs on
Splash's base layer instead of the one it came with:

  h3 metal/MetalBackend: device, queue, metallib, pipeline cache, buffers and
    command buffers -> metal/MetalBackend. engine/Encoder.hpp records a
    command's dispatches as the engine's kernels bind them; a kernel that runs
    an exact grid takes ComputeDispatch::gridThreads. One command runs a DiT
    block, a VAE clip or a preview chunk, the next encoded while it runs.
  h3 model/Safetensors, model/Json and its pread loops -> model/
    SafetensorsCheckpoint (a converted directory read as one index, so the
    DiT and FL2VA manifests' file maps are not read), model/WeightSource and
    parallelFor (model/WeightImages.hpp); the decoders' manifests through
    NSJSONSerialization (model/WeightFiles.hpp).
  h3 engine/Status (codes, H3_TRY, Logger, H3_LOG) -> exceptions, as Splash
    fails (engine/Errors.hpp; CApi.mm returns each as its h3_status), and
    logLine on stderr.
  std::chrono::steady_clock -> AwakeClock.

Gone with the h3 tools that used them: the VAE stage profile, the prep's
intermediate dump and the numpy readers no caller uses. h3_forward_stats keeps
its fields; those one command per block cannot measure (GPU time by step
class, gaps between command buffers) are 0.

h3 itself keeps its own base layer for its CLI, benchmarks and tests, so the
copy no longer follows it file by file. To bring an upstream change,
dev/tools/vendor_video.sh H3_CHECKOUT copies the kernels, ABI headers and
converters again and prints the upstream changes to h3's host code since the
commit above, which are ported into runtime/video/h3 by hand; then record the
new commit here, rebuild, and run make test-engine-metal and make
test-video-real.

Splash's files beside the copy: VideoRuntime.* (the engine process of a video
model, which drives the engine through its C ABI), TextEncoder.* and
TextEncoderABI.h with kernels/video/text_encoder.metal (the prompt's text
encoder), and this note.

Build (Makefile): the kernels compile into splash.metallib as its video
group with Splash's production Metal flags, the prep kernels also without
fast math, as h3 builds them, since they reproduce MLX's arithmetic bit for
bit. The host code compiles with Splash's flags plus -ffp-contract=off, as h3
builds it, into build/engine/libsplash-video.a, which links before the
engine's library; the mp4 writer links AVFoundation, CoreMedia and CoreVideo.

